::PC_U03_L01_GP01::
Question ID: PC_U03_L01_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.7
Question:
Convert 180 degrees to radians.
{
=A. pi radians#Correct. 180 degrees equals pi radians.
~B. 2pi radians#2pi radians equals 360 degrees, not 180 degrees.
~C. 90 radians#This keeps a degree-size number instead of converting to radians.
~D. 180pi radians#This multiplies by pi without dividing by 180.
}
::PC_U03_L01_GP02::
Question ID: PC_U03_L01_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.7
Question:
Which angle is coterminal with 410 degrees and between 0 and 360 degrees?
{
~A. 410 degrees#410 degrees is greater than 360 degrees, so it is not in the requested interval.
=B. 50 degrees#Correct. 410 - 360 = 50 degrees.
~C. -50 degrees#This is not coterminal with 410 degrees in the requested interval.
~D. 770 degrees#This adds another rotation instead of finding the angle between 0 and 360 degrees.
}
::PC_U03_L01_GP03::
Question ID: PC_U03_L01_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.7
Question:
Convert pi/2 radians to degrees.
{
~A. 45 degrees#pi/4 radians equals 45 degrees.
~B. 60 degrees#pi/3 radians equals 60 degrees.
=C. 90 degrees#Correct. pi/2 radians equals 90 degrees.
~D. 180 degrees#pi radians equals 180 degrees.
}
::PC_U03_L01_GP04::
Question ID: PC_U03_L01_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.7
Question:
Which operation helps find a positive coterminal angle for -120 degrees?
{
~A. Multiply by pi#Multiplying by pi is part of conversion, not coterminal angle adjustment.
~B. Divide by 180#Dividing by 180 is part of conversion, not coterminal angle adjustment.
~C. Subtract 360 degrees#Subtracting 360 degrees makes the angle more negative.
=D. Add 360 degrees#Correct. Adding 360 degrees gives 240 degrees, which is coterminal and positive.
}
::PC_U03_L01_GP05::
Question ID: PC_U03_L01_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.TR.7
Question:
How many radians are in one full rotation?
{
=A. 2pi radians#Correct. One full rotation is 2pi radians.
~B. pi radians#pi radians is half a rotation.
~C. 360pi radians#This incorrectly multiplies degree measure by pi.
~D. 1/2 pi radians#1/2 pi radians is a quarter rotation.
}